sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/smallest-universal-turing-machine-2-3--grok-4-7--1> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Prove Smith's tape is not eventually periodic" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/smallest-universal-turing-machine-2-3> ;
    dct:description "Encode Wolfram machine 596440 exactly as published: state 1 on colors 0, 1, 2 writes (1, right, state 2), (2, left, state 1), (1, left, state 1); state 2 writes (2, left, state 1), (2, right, state 2), (0, right, state 1). Define eventually periodic as the existence of a center bound N and periods p, q such that the tape repeats with period p to the right of N and period q to the left. Define finite support as the case where both periodic parts are the blank symbol. Formalize Smith's doubling recurrence for the infinite initial condition, as described in his prize proof and in the public development at the Wolfram Institute Lean notes, and prove that this particular tape is not eventually periodic. Separately audit that public development against this repo's rules: no sorry, no extra axioms. The audit's job is to isolate the infinite-tape hypothesis, not to re-prove universality under a periodic background." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A no-sorry lemma that Smith's infinite initial condition is not eventually periodic, plus an axiom audit showing his universality theorem uses that tape." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The doubling recurrence in fact produces an eventually periodic tape, or the public development's universality theorem does not depend on a non-periodic background. Either outcome would change the status of the objection rather than confirm it." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define machine 596440 and the two tape restrictions as Lean predicates. 2. Transcribe Smith's doubling construction of the infinite initial tape. 3. Prove that tape is not eventually periodic. 4. Audit the public Lean development and list every hypothesis it still assumes, in particular the non-periodic background. 5. Reject the module if universality is stated without that hypothesis." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): hard" ;
    skos:note "Missing: The public Lean notes list open items. Closing those items under a no-sorry rule may exceed a month even though the non-periodicity lemma itself is the smaller target." ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-27> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-28> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-23> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://wolframinstitute.github.io/TuringMachine/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.wolframscience.com/prizes/tm23/technicaldetails.html> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.25088/ComplexSystems.29.1.1> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/smallest-universal-turing-machine-2-3> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Settle whether the (2,3) Turing machine is universal under a conventional definition" .

  <https://wolframinstitute.github.io/TuringMachine/> dct:title "Wolfram (2,3) universality in Lean" ;
    dct:description "Public development claiming theorems Smith.wolfram23_universal_ic and Smith.wolfram23_infinite_ic; used as the artifact to audit, not as a finished no-sorry proof." .
  <https://www.wolframscience.com/prizes/tm23/technicaldetails.html> dct:title "Wolfram 2,3 Turing machine technical details" ;
    dct:description "Transition table of machine 596440." .
  <https://doi.org/10.25088/ComplexSystems.29.1.1> dct:title "Universality of Wolfram’s 2, 3 Turing Machine" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Smith 2020, Universality of Wolfram's 2, 3 Turing Machine" .
}

